From c406241f484f568dbc4fb03fb5594df1d8282011 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:37:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: Dolby Vision sent as HDR10+, the next vblank
 by the last flip where the vblank cannot be read

The first picture of a scene waits for its own measurement until 4 ms
before the next vblank, which it found with the vblank ioctl; nvidia-drm has
no vblank with its defaults, so on NVIDIA graphics it never waited. Every
driver flips with the out-fence of its commit, signalled at the flip with the
kernel's time of it, on the clock of the vblanks: the flips are on the grid
of the vblanks.

CDVPlusClock (DVPlusClock.h, nothing of DRM in it) takes the vblank where the
ioctl gives one, else the last flip whose time was read, with the period of
the mode it flipped in; every flip anchors the grid again, and nothing is
taken from the flips after one that did not come or whose time could not be
read, after a commit that changes the mode, after a new video, or in a mode
of another period. While Dolby Vision goes out as HDR10+, PresentRender()
reads the time of every flip from the copy of its out-fence that the
measurement of when frames flip already takes (CPresentLag), and tells it the
flips that did not come and the commits of a mode; the same 4 ms margin.
Where neither tells where the vblanks are, the previous scene's metadata, as
before, and kodi.log says so once. The line of every 240 frames says how many
first pictures were timed by the vblank and how many by the last flip.

Host test (dvplus_clock_test.cpp): 15 checks, the two sources, the vblank
taken over a flip, a flip that anchors again with its own phase, a flip that
did not come or could not be read, another period, a stale flip; 5 mutations
of DVPlusClock.h, all caught. Not run on a display: that the out-fence of
nvidia-drm is signalled with the time of its flip is read in DRM, not
verified on NVIDIA's driver.
